ck_revision":"d4c7bc701f255f168891a977","time_limit":25},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_joint_cdf_discrete_06992fea_004","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"For a discrete pair $(X,Y)$, a student checks $F_{X,Y}(2,3)=F_X(2)F_Y(3)$ at this one threshold pair. What does this check establish?","options":["The variables must be independent.","Independence remains undetermined.","The variables must be dependent.","The variables must have identical marginal distributions."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"35cbccd49de5194e995876c8","time_limit":25},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_joint_cdf_discrete_06992fea_005","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"For a discrete pair $(X,Y)$, suppose $F_X(2)=0.60$ and $F_Y(4)=0.35$. Which upper bound on $F_{X,Y}(2,4)$ is guaranteed without assuming independence?","options":["$F_{X,Y}(2,4)\\le0.35$","$F_{X,Y}(2,4)\\le0.21$","$F_{X,Y}(2,4)\\le0.05$","$F_{X,Y}(2,4)\\le0.25$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"7a3d36adfdebd97fea970785","time_limit":25},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_joint_cdf_discrete_06992fea_007","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"A pair has masses $p(0,0)=0.1$, $p(0,1)=0.2$, $p(0,2)=0.1$, $p(1,0)=0.15$, $p(1,1)=0.25$, $p(1,2)=0.2$, with $p(x,y)=\\mathbb{P}(X=x,Y=y)$. What is $F_{X,Y}(0,1)$?","options":["$0.3$","$0.2$","$0.4$","$0.7$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"7982b56697a11446f586778b","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_joint_cdf_discrete_06992fea_008","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Let $X$ be uniform on $\\{1,2,3\\}$ and $Y=X$. What is $F_{X,Y}(2,2)$?","options":["$\\tfrac{4}{9}$","$\\tfrac{1}{3}$","$\\tfrac{1}{9}$","$\\tfrac{2}{3}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"d8ea6de9eddd261b9551249f","time_limit":25},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_joint_cdf_discrete_06992fea_009","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"For integer-valued $X$ and $Y$, suppose $F(2,2)=0.6$, $F(1,2)=0.4$, $F(2,1)=0.35$ and $F(1,1)=0.25$, where $F=F_{X,Y}$. What is $p_{X,Y}(2,2)$?","options":["$0.35$","$0.2$","$0.1$","$0.25$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"d7424e3b5aa927e850cb9a02","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_joint_cdf_discrete_06992fea_010","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Suppose $F_X(1)=0.5$, $F_Y(1)=0.4$ and $F_{X,Y}(1,1)=0.3$. What is $\\mathbb{P}(X>1,\\,Y>1)$?","options":["$0.7$","$0.4$","$0.1$","$0.3$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"8ee63cc54878af2a50735b21","time_limit":20},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_joint_cdf_discrete_06992fea_011","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Let $X$ be integer-valued and $Y$ discrete, with joint CDF $F=F_{X,Y}$. Write $F(a,2-)=\\lim_{t\\uparrow2}F(a,t)$. Which expression equals $\\mathbb{P}(X=3,Y<2)$?","options":["$F(3,2)-F(2,2)$","$F(3,2-)-F(2,2)$","$F(3,2-)-F(2,2-)$","$F(3,2)-F(2,2-)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"1f4bc4a61175b3032e868794","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_joint_cdf_discrete_06992fea_012","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"For integer-valued $X$ and $Y$, which identity holds without any independence assumption?","options":["$F_{X,Y}(2.8,-0.2)=F_{X,Y}(3,0)$","$F_{X,Y}(2.8,-0.2)=F_{X,Y}(2,-1)$","$F_{X,Y}(2.8,-0.2)=F_{X,Y}(2,0)$","$F_{X,Y}(2.8,-0.2)=F_{X,Y}(3,-1)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"5fb800cf23f474c82d17b098","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_joint_cdf_discrete_06992fea_013","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Let $F=F_{X,Y}$ be the joint CDF of a discrete pair. Suppose $a<b$ and $c<d$. What is guaranteed about $F(a,d)$ and $F(b,c)$?","options":["Either value can be larger.","$F(a,d)\\le F(b,c)$ always.","$F(a,d)\\ge F(b,c)$ always.","$F(a,d)=F(b,c)$ always."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"8d72b28ebe3b96ca74747f7c","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_joint_cdf_discrete_06992fea_014","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Two independent fair six-sided dice show $X$ and $Y$. With $F=F_{X,Y}$, what is $\\mathbb{P}(1<X\\le4,\\ 2<Y\\le5)$?","options":["$\\tfrac{7}{36}$","$\\tfrac{1}{4}$","$\\tfrac{1}{3}$","$\\tfrac{5}{9}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"2addd52dcdf3b0d775ad8804","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_joint_cdf_discrete_06992fea_015","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"A fair six-sided die shows $X$ and an independent fair four-sided die shows $Y$. Using the joint CDF, what is $\\mathbb{P}(\\max(X,Y)\\le3)$?","options":["$\\tfrac{1}{2}$","$\\tfrac{7}{8}$","$\\tfrac{5}{24}$","$\\tfrac{3}{8}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"ab315ffc6460168b00872e42","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_joint_cdf_discrete_06992fea_017","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"A proposed joint CDF $F$ is nondecreasing in each coordinate. For real $a<b$ and $c<d$, write $R=F(b,d)-F(a,d)-F(b,c)+F(a,c)$. Which additional requirement follows from interpreting $R$ as a probability?","options":["$R=\\mathbb{P}(a<X\\le b)\\mathbb{P}(c<Y\\le d)$","$R=\\mathbb{P}(a<X\\le b,c<Y\\le d)\\ge0$","$R=\\mathbb{P}(a<X\\le b)+\\mathbb{P}(c<Y\\le d)$","$R=\\mathbb{P}(a<X\\le b\\text{ or }c<Y\\le d)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"7d853b80f60dd3231d89a918","time_limit":40},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_joint_cdf_discrete_06992fea_018","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"For a discrete pair $(X,Y)$, suppose $F_X(2)=0.80$ and $F_Y(5)=0.70$. As the joint distribution varies subject to these two probabilities, which is the exact range of possible values of $F_{X,Y}(2,5)$?","options":["$[0,0.56]$","$[0.56,0.70]$","$[0.50,0.70]$","$[0,0.70]$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"35d20d4619f17af0f450be42","time_limit":40},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_joint_cdf_discrete_06992fea_019","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"Two independent fair six-sided dice are rolled. Let $U$ be the smaller score and $V$ the larger. What is $\\mathbb{P}(U\\le2,V\\le5)$?","options":["$\\tfrac{25}{36}$","$\\tfrac{5}{9}$","$\\tfrac{10}{36}$","$\\tfrac{4}{9}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"d9c8bbb648f5df1f077ae43a","time_limit":40},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_joint_cdf_discrete_06992fea_020","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"Integer-valued $X$ and $Y$ have joint CDF $F(x,y)=(1-2^{-x})(1-3^{-y})$ for integers $x,y\\ge1$, and $F=0$ if $x<1$ or $y<1$. What is $\\mathbb{P}(X=Y)$?","options":["$\\tfrac{2}{5}$","$\\tfrac{1}{5}$","$\\tfrac{1}{3}$","$\\tfrac{3}{5}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"c1f41c6a2863eb39e3ab711b","time_limit":40}]}}